==============================================================================
H038 -- DARK-MATTER DENSITY PROFILE FROM THE AMPLITUDE LAW + HYDROSTATICS
==============================================================================
  M_b = 6.00e+10 Msun, Hernquist a = 2.40 kpc, R_eff = 4.36 kpc
  NFW comparison halo: M200 = 1.0e+12 Msun, c = 12, r200 = 211.6 kpc, r_s = 17.63 kpc

==============================================================================
PART 1 -- CONTROL: DIFFERENTIATING THE AMPLITUDE LAW GIVES THE STANDARD FORM
==============================================================================
  M_ph(<r) = M_b r/r_M  =>  rho = (1/4 pi r^2) dM/dr = M_b/(4 pi r_M r^2)
  standard form                                     = sqrt(G M_b a0)/(4 pi G r^2)
    canonical    a0=9.3619e-11  r=   5.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 9.453 kpc
    canonical    a0=9.3619e-11  r=  10.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 9.453 kpc
    canonical    a0=9.3619e-11  r=  30.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 9.453 kpc
    canonical    a0=9.3619e-11  r= 100.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 9.453 kpc
    alternative  a0=1.1279e-10  r=   5.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 8.612 kpc
    alternative  a0=1.1279e-10  r=  10.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 8.612 kpc
    alternative  a0=1.1279e-10  r=  30.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 8.612 kpc
    alternative  a0=1.1279e-10  r= 100.0 kpc  ratio(lhs/rhs) = 1.000000000000   r_M = 8.612 kpc
  [PASS] C1 [CONTROL] dM_ph/dr from the amplitude law = standard phantom form
         measured: max |ratio-1| over 2 footings x 4 radii = 2.220e-16
         threshold: 1e-12.  Both are  M_b/r_M /(4 pi r^2) with M_b/r_M = sqrt(G M_b a0)/G.  This is an algebraic identity, printed as a control, not counted as a finding.

==============================================================================
PART 2 -- HYDROSTATIC EQUILIBRIUM FORCES gamma = 2 AND THEN FIXES sigma^2
==============================================================================
  rho = A r^-gamma, self-gravity only:
    LHS dlnrho/dlnr term exponent  = -gamma-1
    RHS (-r g/ sigma^2) exponent   = -2 gamma + 1
        gamma   LHS exp   RHS exp   residual
         0.50    -1.500     0.000     -1.500
         1.00    -2.000    -1.000     -1.000
         1.50    -2.500    -2.000     -0.500
         1.80    -2.800    -2.600     -0.200
         2.00    -3.000    -3.000      0.000
         2.20    -3.200    -3.400      0.200
         3.00    -4.000    -5.000      1.000
  [PASS] C2 [DERIVED] an isothermal power-law halo in equilibrium against its own
         measured: residual(gamma=2) = 0.000e+00; residual(1.5) = -0.500; residual(3.0) = 1.000
         threshold: |residual(2)| < 1e-15 AND |residual(off-2)| > 0.4.  gamma = 2 is forced by the r-exponents, INDEPENDENTLY of sigma^2 -- so the isothermal equilibrium is a one-parameter family A = sigma^2/(2 pi G), and the temperature is NOT derived by hydrostatics alone.

  coefficient match: 2 sigma^2 A r^-3 = 4 pi G A^2 r^-3  =>  A = sigma^2/(2 pi G)
  amplitude law fixes A = M_b/(4 pi r_M); so hydrostatic => sigma^2 = 2 pi G A:
    canonical    r_M =   9.453 kpc | sigma^2 derived = 1.365335e+10 m^2/s^2 | registered = 1.365335e+10 | rel = 2.220e-16
                 sigma derived =  116.848 km/s ; v_flat = (G M_b a0)^(1/4) =  165.247 km/s ; sigma/v_flat = 0.707107
    alternative  r_M =   8.612 kpc | sigma^2 derived = 1.498623e+10 m^2/s^2 | registered = 1.498623e+10 | rel = 0.000e+00
                 sigma derived =  122.418 km/s ; v_flat = (G M_b a0)^(1/4) =  173.126 km/s ; sigma/v_flat = 0.707107
  [PASS] C3 [CONSISTENCY CLOSURE, NOT INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE] hydrostatic equilibrium +
      the amplitude law reproduce the registered temperature exactly
         measured: canonical: rel=2.22e-16 ; alternative: rel=0.00e+00
         threshold: rel < 1e-12 on both footings.  HONEST TAG: sigma^2 = 2 pi G A and A = M_b/(4 pi r_M) make the registered temperature and the amplitude law the SAME EQUATION under hydrostatic equilibrium.  This removes sigma^2 as a free parameter; it is NOT new evidence for either relation separately.

==============================================================================
PART 3 -- THE TOTAL-GRAVITY CORRECTION: THE AMPLITUDE LAW IS THE ASYMPTOTE
==============================================================================
  Now support the phantom against baryons + itself (Newtonian total field):
      dln rho/dln r = -(G/sigma^2) [M_b(<r) + M_ph(<r)]/r ,  G/sigma^2 = 2 r_M/M_b
  With the REGISTERED constant sigma^2 the equilibrium profile integrates to
      rho_ph = [M_b/(4 pi r_M)] r^-2 exp(2 r_M/(r+a))       (Hernquist baryons)
  i.e. the amplitude law is the r >> r_M limit; the correction is O(r_M/r).

   r [kpc]   r/r_M  rho_B/rho_A  gamma_ph(A)  gamma_ph(B) sigma^2(r)/sigma^2_reg
      10.0    1.06        4.593       -2.000       -3.230                  1.615
      15.0    1.59        2.964       -2.000       -2.937                  1.468
      20.0    2.12        2.326       -2.000       -2.754                  1.377
      30.0    3.17        1.792       -2.000       -2.540                  1.270
      50.0    5.29        1.434       -2.000       -2.344                  1.172
      80.0    8.46        1.258       -2.000       -2.223                  1.111
     100.0   10.58        1.203       -2.000       -2.180                  1.090
     200.0   21.16        1.098       -2.000       -2.092                  1.046
  [FAIL] C4 [NEGATIVE / INTERNAL TENSION, QUANTIFIED] a CONSTANT sigma^2 is
         measured: rho_B/rho_A = 4.593 at r = 10 kpc (= r_M) and 1.792 at 30 kpc (= 3.2 r_M); sigma^2(r=10kpc)/sigma^2_reg = 1.615
         threshold: rho_B/rho_A < 1.10 at r = r_M (i.e. the two readings agree).  MEASURED: they do NOT -- with the constant registered temperature the equilibrium profile is several times denser than the amplitude law near r_M.  MECHANISM: the registered sigma^2 is exactly the SELF-GRAVITY closure, so it supports the phantom against its own field only.  The two readings coincide only asymptotically (r >> r_M).  Keeping the amplitude law under total gravity requires a RADIUS-DEPENDENT sigma^2, sigma^2(r) = (G/2)[M_b(<r)/r + M_b/r_M], which is larger inward.

==============================================================================
PART 3b -- WHICH READING DOES THE MILKY WAY ROTATION CURVE SELECT?
==============================================================================
  r = 20 kpc, M_b = 6.0e+10 Msun (canonical footing):
    reading A (amplitude law)          v =  193.9 km/s
    reading B (constant sigma^2, total) v =  263.3 km/s
    measured (Eilers+2019 Gaia DR2, v(8.12 kpc)=229, slope -1.7 km/s/kpc)
                                       v =  208.8 +- 15 km/s
  baryon mass each reading needs at 20 kpc:  A: 7.56e+10 Msun ; B: 2.92e+10 Msun
  measured MW baryons: ~5e10 stars + ~1e10 gas = 6e10 Msun (range ~4.5-8e10).
  [PASS] C4b [DECISION] the MW rotation curve at 20 kpc selects one reading
         measured: v_A = 193.9 km/s, v_B = 263.3 km/s, measured 208.8 +- 15 km/s; implied M_b: A = 7.56e+10, B = 2.92e+10 Msun
         threshold: reading A within 15% of the measurement AND reading B more than 20% away.  Systematics stated: the MW baryon mass is uncertain by ~+-25%, which moves v_A by ~+-7%; reading B's offset is ~28%, well outside that.  VERDICT: the amplitude law (A) is the reading the data selects; the constant-sigma^2 total-gravity reading (B) over-predicts the MW rotation speed at 20 kpc and is excluded.  So the O(r_M/r) correction of Part 3 is a genuine internal tension, resolved in favour of A: the phantom is supported against its own field, not against the baryons.

